This file is not an "official" part of MiNT, but please distribute it
with bgacc.acc
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
BGACC: a background program accessory for MiNT
Written by Eric R. Smith (eric.smith@uwo.ca)

This program is in the public domain. Do with it what you will. As a
courtesy, please include these docs with all distributions, and give
proper credit to the author.

See the BUGS section below, please!

"bgacc" is a simple little accessory that gives an easy way of running
TOS programs in the background of a GEM session. Selecting this accessory
pops up a file selector box; use this box to select the program you want
to run. This should *not* be a GEM program; you already have a GEM program
running, or you wouldn't be able to get to the menu bar! GEM will likely
become horribly confused if there are two programs making GEM calls
simultaneously.

Once you've selected the program you want, it'll be run. To return to
whatever GEM program you were using, hold the Control key down and press
the "0" key on the keypad (the long one next to the "." :-). The TOS program
will continue to run in the background; you can return to it by again
selecting "Background Prg" from the desk accessory menu. If you exit from
the background program, there will be a brief pause and the file selector
box will appear again so that you can select another program to run.

BUGS:
It appears that bgacc somehow causes GEM to crash if you change
resolutions :-(.

HINTS:
Note that you have to have MiNT in your auto folder, and run GEM manually,
to make this work (i.e. GEM has to start up under MiNT, not vice-versa).
Thus, it won't work on a TT, since you have to start MiNT after GEM on
the TT. If MiNT isn't active when you boot up, the accessory will not
install itself.

Some graphics oriented programs don't actually make GEM AES calls, and
these can be run from bgacc. Problems are less likely to occur if you
run the program to completion. If you want to try experimenting, do it
sometime when you're not doing anything important, and be prepared to
reboot if you hang up GEM.
